{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/cyexa1","slideIndex":0,"text":"Background slide, not a report of the present experiments. The top photographs are user-supplied illustrations of an actual grasp. The editable chart is a qualitative schematic, with arbitrary aperture units and movement progress; its values and peak timing are not empirical estimates.\n[5] Jeannerod M (1984). The Timing of Natural Prehension Movements. Journal of Motor Behavior 16(3):235–254. https://doi.org/10.1080/00222895.1984.10735319 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15151851/\n[6] Smeets JBJ, Brenner E (1999). A New View on Grasping. Motor Control 3(3):237–271. https://doi.org/10.1123/mcj.3.3.237 This paper integrates previous experimental findings with a theoretical alternative.\n[7] Wing AM, Turton A, Fraser C (1986). Grasp Size and Accuracy of Approach in Reaching. Journal of Motor Behavior 18(3):245–260. https://doi.org/10.1080/00222895.1986.10735380\nNo universal numerical claims about maximum-aperture timing or speed-related percentage increases are made. This slide does not claim that all grasping is unconscious."}